WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:06.400
If you're trying to make sense of web application architecture, you've no doubt heard of Apache and Nginx.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:13.199
They're both free open source projects, very commonly used as HTTP and reverse proxy servers.

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.440
Let's unpack those definitions a little bit.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:24.532
Say I'm sitting at home with a web browser going to my favorite website, ibm.com, of course!

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:35.369
It's easy to imagine that my browser is talking to the IBM server and asking for the right web page.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:37.560
That's the request right there.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.369
Now, in this case, let's say it's asking for cloud security.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:48.899
And the web server goes around and digs through its data to put that page together and then ships it back to me.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:59.729
So here's the the resulting web page nicely rendered in my browser so I can learn all about hybrid multi-cloud security solutions.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.670
A perfect afternoon activity.

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:09.080
Now, this is an example of a simple web server--software which can accept HTTP connections

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:15.180
and map them to documents or images or other resources and then return them to the requester.

00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.310
That's me, or at least my web browser.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.998
And this may have been an accurate diagram back in the early 2000s,

00:01:22.998 --> 00:01:34.438
but most public-facing websites get so much traffic that it makes sense to have a couple of web servers back here serving that content.

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.459
And they're all capable of delivering the same content.

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:46.620
And then this thing becomes a load balancer upfront to make sure that no one server gets too busy.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:59.557
Some load balancers operate as layer 4 load balancers, and that means that they work at the transport level, and they simply route the request to each available server.

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:03.980
So they can handle DNS, mail, TCP and UDP traffic.

00:02:04.610 --> 00:02:11.389
Now, I personally like my load balancers the way I like my bean dip: 7 layer.

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:23.620
And well, Apache and Nginx are often used as 7 layer load balancers, operating the application layer, specifically HTTP.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:29.119
So this specific request comes in, it hits the proxy server up front

00:02:29.119 --> 00:02:36.379
and it goes and initiates its own connection with the server, or the servers on the private network, to fulfill the request.

00:02:36.650 --> 00:02:40.279
So we're talking to those servers by proxy.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:43.880
Now, this gives us several key advantages.

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:45.727
So let's take a look at some of those.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:51.689
And the first one is all about load balancing.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:59.682
And I think it's the most obvious advantage because we can route the requests to any number of the servers now,

00:02:59.682 --> 00:03:05.639
we're no longer constricted to the performance of a single server-- we can send it to the one that is least busy.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:09.839
Number 2 is security.

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:17.090
Now, we're never letting the outside world know what's back here at all.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:19.536
All they ever see is this, the reverse proxy.

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:28.909
Even though it's just reaching out and tasking those servers with the work to the requester, the web browser, it looks like it's all coming from right here.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:34.159
It takes all the credit, which is kind of rude, now that I think about it, but we'll let it slide.

00:03:34.310 --> 00:03:42.044
Now, although it sees the same request over and over again, if that is for a static non-dynamic resource,

00:03:42.044 --> 00:03:49.485
let's say a very popular image is at the top of every web page, it can do something called caching.

00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:58.279
So it can cache that resource and then it can serve it up on its own, saving time and network throughput.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:03:59.990
So at least it's trying to help there.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:08.289
And speaking of reducing network throughput, it can also optimize compression, and that is benefit number 4.

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:16.569
And that compression is specifically between itself and the client, decreasing load times.

00:04:17.350 --> 00:04:29.150
And it can also handle SSL connections out to the web, allowing servers on a private protected network to communicate in plain text,

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:32.359
which can dramatically speed up some applications.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:40.660
Now, typically we recommend employing encryption everywhere, but in some cases it's not practical and it's not needed.

00:04:41.110 --> 00:04:46.809
So I just wanted to put that disclaimer out there so I don't get yelled at.

00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:59.142
Okay okay, now, the point here, we came here to talk about Apache and we came here to talk about and Nginx.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:04.360
And they-- Apache and Nginx --they do all this.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:11.110
The difference to know is that Apache, well, Apache came first.

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.019
And it's been around for a long time.

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:16.840
We're talking pre-2000 here.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.910
And it started out as a basic web server, which has been extended over time with the use of modules.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:30.199
So we want to enable the mod_proxy and mod_http modules to give it reverse proxy capabilities.

00:05:30.650 --> 00:05:39.273
It works, it performs well, and it's got a ton of functionality besides-- and in addition to --being a web or reverse proxy server.

00:05:40.069 --> 00:05:50.449
But that, that wasn't good enough for Igor Sysoev, who released Nginx in 2004 with the explicit goal of outperforming Apache.

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:53.779
Nothing like having a target painting on your back, huh?

00:05:54.080 --> 00:06:01.609
So suffice to say, Nginx does outperform Apache as a simple web server and proxy server.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:05.269
It is without question fast.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:17.149
It also has a very simple configuration, and that makes it very easy to handle large volumes of transactions, especially when it comes to simple static files.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:29.529
When we start getting into dynamic content, because Apache has its roots as an extensible server, that's where it really holds an edge.

00:06:29.560 --> 00:06:34.360
There's just a lot more you can do with it, albeit at the expense of speed in some cases.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:39.970
And I think it's also important to note here that we're talking about HTTP traffic.

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:47.079
And it's not just web pages like here, but any HTTP traffic, including all of those REST calls.

00:06:47.080 --> 00:06:49.893
Cause you are you did watch my video about REST calls, right?

00:06:49.893 --> 00:06:58.000
Well, they're handling these REST calls which power just about every web and mobile app there is.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:02.040
And right now Nginx is a bit more popular in the container space.

00:07:02.350 --> 00:07:06.609
So it enjoys a bit of a popularity boost from all those containerized solutions.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.892
But as we've seen, this is a hotly contested space.

00:07:10.270 --> 00:07:13.729
And they don't need to fight-- they can be friends!

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:22.810
It's entirely possible to have Nginx acting as your reverse proxy with several Apache web servers behind it, or vice versa.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:25.389
The fundamentals are largely the same.

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:29.619
It really does come down to what fits best into your environment.

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:39.970
So whichever solution you go with, you will enjoy a whole host of features that will keep your web data flowing quickly, safely and reliably.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:43.730
If you have any questions, please drop us a line below.

00:07:43.730 --> 00:07:48.350
And if you want to see more videos like this in the future, please Like and Subscribe.

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:49.920
Thanks for watching.

